        "title": "Body Plans: Symmetry, Segmentation, Coelom",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Animal body plans are defined by a small number of architectural variables: symmetry type, tissue layers (grade), body cavity type, and segmentation pattern. These are not arbitrary — they represent solutions to the mechanical and physiological problems of being a multicellular heterotroph. The deep convergences (multiple independent origins of eyes, limbs, segmentation) and the constraints (all large aquatic bilaterians need a coelom) reveal the physics of animal design."
